# Line endings are part of the payload here, not a formatting preference.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-11 on a real windows-latest runner: both Windows cells of the CI matrix
# failed `gb-surface-gate.py --selftest` at 49/54, and only the five known-good* fixtures
# failed. Root cause: Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so `actions/checkout`
# rewrote LF to CRLF in every `fixtures/**/pages/*.md`, while `gb-surface-gate.py` hashes the
# file's RAW BYTES against a manifest digest computed over LF. Every page mismatched.
#
# The gate is right to hash raw bytes — it is a fetch-integrity check, and normalising before
# hashing would make it blind to exactly the corruption it exists to catch. The defect is that
# git was permitted to rewrite the bytes under it. So the fix belongs here, at the point where
# the mutation happens, not in the checker.
#
# Reproduced and cured on macOS without a Windows machine, because core.autocrlf is a git
# setting rather than an OS one:
#   git -c core.autocrlf=true  clone <repo>  ->  SELFTEST FAIL — 49/54 fixtures
#   git -c core.autocrlf=false clone <repo>  ->  SELFTEST PASS — 54/54 fixtures
# 49/54 is the exact figure both Windows cells produced, which is what makes it the same
# defect rather than a lookalike.

# The fixture corpus is content-addressed: never translate it, on any platform.
fixtures/** -text

# Artifacts and manifests are hashed or byte-compared the same way.
*.json -text
*.jsonl -text

# The hero is binary; say so rather than relying on detection.
*.jpg binary
*.png binary
